WEBVTT

1
00:00:21,555 --> 00:00:25,959
["It Was Love At First Sight"
by Wesley Bright plays]

2
00:00:40,308 --> 00:00:42,075
[Sera] The one on the left
is Endless Sun.

3
00:00:42,076 --> 00:00:44,744
- [John] Mm-hmm.
- It contains grains of sand from the beaches of Mauritius.

4
00:00:44,745 --> 00:00:46,913
That's what gives it
that, like, nice texture.

5
00:00:46,914 --> 00:00:48,448
- Okay.
- Then the one on the right,

6
00:00:48,449 --> 00:00:50,550
that one's Parisian Gold.

7
00:00:50,551 --> 00:00:52,585
The pigment comes
from a gilding house in France

8
00:00:52,586 --> 00:00:54,254
that supplied
the Palace of Versailles

9
00:00:54,255 --> 00:00:55,655
with cornicing
for the Hall of Mirrors.

10
00:00:55,656 --> 00:00:57,090
- Really?
- I know. I know, yes.

11
00:00:57,091 --> 00:00:58,691
- Okay.
- Yeah.

12
00:00:58,692 --> 00:01:00,627
[John] Hmm.

13
00:01:00,628 --> 00:01:02,929
Is that a hard "no"
on Tahitian Vanilla?

14
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:04,931
- Yes, because that's an ice cream flavor.
- Okay.

15
00:01:04,932 --> 00:01:07,500
- No. Baby, this is our baby's room.
- Okay.

16
00:01:07,501 --> 00:01:09,169
I want it to be perfect.

17
00:01:09,170 --> 00:01:10,870
I know you do.

18
00:01:10,871 --> 00:01:12,439
I'm just gonna say
it'd be easier

19
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:14,374
if we knew if it was gonna be
a boy or a girl.

20
00:01:14,375 --> 00:01:16,143
And we'll know in a few months.

21
00:01:16,144 --> 00:01:18,511
- Besides, I'm not refusing to find out.
- Yeah?

22
00:01:18,512 --> 00:01:20,580
- Just that I can live with not knowing.
- Hmm.

23
00:01:20,581 --> 00:01:22,683
The fact that you can't is...

24
00:01:23,151 --> 00:01:24,717
- Ah, it's so much fun.
- [chuckles]

25
00:01:24,718 --> 00:01:27,520
- Yeah, I'm getting that whole vibe over here.
- Mm-hmm.

26
00:01:27,521 --> 00:01:29,789
[chuckles] What do you think?

27
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:31,057
Um...

28
00:01:31,058 --> 00:01:32,559
[whispers] Parisian Gold.

29
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:35,362
- Oh, I think I like the Parisian Gold.
- Oh, good.

30
00:01:35,363 --> 00:01:37,364
- Me too.
- Settled.

31
00:01:37,365 --> 00:01:38,999
[John chuckles]

32
00:01:43,571 --> 00:01:45,372
[John chuckles] Oh.

33
00:01:45,373 --> 00:01:46,940
You're next.

34
00:01:49,543 --> 00:01:52,446
You've been pretty incredible
through this whole thing.

35
00:01:53,981 --> 00:01:56,649
With all the doctors
and all the injections

36
00:01:56,650 --> 00:01:58,886
and all that craziness,
you've been amazing.

37
00:02:01,289 --> 00:02:03,390
Well, I didn't do it alone.

38
00:02:03,391 --> 00:02:06,193
No, I did it alone. A couple.

39
00:02:06,194 --> 00:02:08,962
- [chuckles]
- Yep, all by myself.

40
00:02:11,765 --> 00:02:12,766
You ready?

41
00:02:15,636 --> 00:02:17,637
Yeah, I am. I'm ready.

42
00:02:17,638 --> 00:02:20,441
I mean, I'm terrified,
but I'm ready.

43
00:02:22,075 --> 00:02:23,744
You love me.

44
00:02:24,412 --> 00:02:26,447
You love me too.

45
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:36,856
[Sera] <i>Logic and statistical
reasoning appear to live</i>

46
00:02:36,857 --> 00:02:41,561
<i>somewhere between exact science
and the infinite unknown.</i>

47
00:02:41,562 --> 00:02:43,797
But nothing could be further
from the truth.

48
00:02:44,565 --> 00:02:48,134
It's really no different from any
other mathematical field of study.

49
00:02:48,135 --> 00:02:49,302
- And in this way...
- Yep.

50
00:02:49,303 --> 00:02:52,340
[Sera] <i>There's only one answer.</i>

51
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:55,074
- Okay.
- Guys, check it out.

52
00:02:55,075 --> 00:02:57,678
[light piano music playing]

53
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:01,113
You know, she and John
used to be a thing.

54
00:03:01,114 --> 00:03:02,849
- [John] Dude.
- Seriously?

55
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:05,051
- It was a long time ago. I'm
happily married now. - [Steven] Mm.

56
00:03:05,052 --> 00:03:06,419
Everybody believes you.

57
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:08,821
- Would you shut up, man?
- [Sera] <i>There's no gray area.</i>

58
00:03:08,822 --> 00:03:11,292
You can't agree to disagree.

59
00:03:11,825 --> 00:03:15,563
It's as binary as one and zero.

60
00:03:17,164 --> 00:03:18,164
Hey.

61
00:03:18,899 --> 00:03:19,999
Hey. [sighs]

62
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:21,902
You're showing
the Scarsdale house today, right?

63
00:03:22,570 --> 00:03:23,703
Yeah.

64
00:03:23,704 --> 00:03:25,873
You mind if I tag along?

65
00:03:27,908 --> 00:03:30,611
Come on. It's the last project
we worked on together.

66
00:03:31,945 --> 00:03:32,913
Oh.

67
00:03:32,914 --> 00:03:35,114
I-I don't think
that's a good idea.

68
00:03:35,115 --> 00:03:39,119
[piano playing]

69
00:04:01,575 --> 00:04:02,910
[Sera gasps]

70
00:04:04,545 --> 00:04:08,549
[grunting, panting]

71
00:04:09,817 --> 00:04:12,252
[ominous music plays]

72
00:04:14,154 --> 00:04:15,221
[line ringing]

73
00:04:15,222 --> 00:04:18,257
Oh, fuck. John, John,
please pick up, please.

74
00:04:18,258 --> 00:04:20,727
[John] All right, guys.
Appreciate it. Nice to see you.

75
00:04:20,728 --> 00:04:21,794
[on voicemail]
<i>Hi, you've reached John Winter.</i>

76
00:04:21,795 --> 00:04:23,063
<i>Please leave me a message.</i>

77
00:04:26,567 --> 00:04:28,869
Oh! [pants]

78
00:04:30,871 --> 00:04:32,905
<i>Hi, you've reached John Winter.</i>

79
00:04:32,906 --> 00:04:36,109
Oh, fuck, please.
Come on, John. [sniffles]

80
00:04:36,810 --> 00:04:40,781
- [line ringing]
- [breathes heavily]

81
00:04:46,019 --> 00:04:47,655
[elevator dings]

82
00:04:52,159 --> 00:04:53,927
[running footsteps]

83
00:04:58,231 --> 00:05:00,066
- [John] Hey.
- Mm.

84
00:05:00,067 --> 00:05:01,200
Come here.

85
00:05:01,201 --> 00:05:03,235
[John breathing deeply]

86
00:05:03,236 --> 00:05:05,205
[Sera sniffling]

87
00:05:08,275 --> 00:05:11,779
[dramatic music plays]

88
00:05:42,543 --> 00:05:43,777
[John] You love me.

89
00:05:50,350 --> 00:05:51,719
You love me too.

90
00:06:00,861 --> 00:06:04,364
[dramatic music continues]

91
00:06:12,540 --> 00:06:14,374
[John sighs]

92
00:06:21,649 --> 00:06:22,983
I can't stay here.

93
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:27,755
I know.

94
00:06:37,431 --> 00:06:40,934
[soft music plays]

95
00:07:05,258 --> 00:07:08,095
[birds chirping]

96
00:07:10,030 --> 00:07:13,200
[dramatic violin music plays]

97
00:07:47,868 --> 00:07:51,038
[dramatic violin music
continues]

98
00:08:33,547 --> 00:08:35,048
[music ends]

99
00:08:38,819 --> 00:08:40,587
[John] Listen, the right thing's
gonna come along,

100
00:08:40,588 --> 00:08:41,954
and when it does, we'll know it.

101
00:08:41,955 --> 00:08:44,156
It's been over a month.

102
00:08:44,157 --> 00:08:46,593
A-and everything's either
completely out of our price range

103
00:08:46,594 --> 00:08:47,860
or it's a teardown.

104
00:08:47,861 --> 00:08:50,362
I promise we're gonna find
the perfect house,

105
00:08:50,363 --> 00:08:52,899
- and we're gonna live the life of our dreams.
- Yeah?

106
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:56,302
[Jeannie] Perhaps you'd be willing
to expand your search area?

107
00:08:56,303 --> 00:08:58,671
Or I could pull a few listings
from the next town over.

108
00:08:58,672 --> 00:09:02,541
No, no. This-this feels right.
This-this feels like home.

109
00:09:02,542 --> 00:09:05,245
Guys, to be honest,
I've sort of run out of options.

110
00:09:06,079 --> 00:09:08,480
But you seem like you really,
really need to live here,

111
00:09:08,481 --> 00:09:11,685
so I'm gonna recommend
you speak to a friend of mine.

112
00:09:12,352 --> 00:09:14,420
<i>Been in this town
for a long time.</i>

113
00:09:14,421 --> 00:09:15,888
<i>He knows everybody,</i>

114
00:09:15,889 --> 00:09:19,026
<i>and he knows
every single piece of property.</i>

115
00:09:20,493 --> 00:09:22,161
<i>If there's anybody
who can help you find</i>

116
00:09:22,162 --> 00:09:24,832
<i>the home you're looking for,
it's him.</i>

117
00:09:26,233 --> 00:09:28,034
[John] Look at this.
It's like the beginning

118
00:09:28,035 --> 00:09:29,355
- of a horror movie.
- [Sera] Tsk.

119
00:09:30,303 --> 00:09:31,938
Hey, honey, I got a great idea.

120
00:09:31,939 --> 00:09:33,706
Let's go visit the dude
we don't know

121
00:09:33,707 --> 00:09:35,975
with the big, scary house
out in the middle of nowhere.

122
00:09:35,976 --> 00:09:37,910
What could possibly go wrong?

123
00:09:37,911 --> 00:09:39,779
You know what? At this point,
we're so desperate,

124
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:40,880
I'll take all the help
we can get.

125
00:09:40,881 --> 00:09:42,382
- Yeah, I guess so.
- Mm-hmm.

126
00:09:43,984 --> 00:09:45,251
- Holy shit.
- [Sera laughs]

127
00:09:45,252 --> 00:09:46,753
Wow.

128
00:09:46,754 --> 00:09:49,957
[light instrumental music
playing]

129
00:10:03,170 --> 00:10:04,170
Welcome.

130
00:10:04,171 --> 00:10:06,505
I'm so glad you decided to come.

131
00:10:06,506 --> 00:10:08,074
I'm Emmett. Emmett Claymore.

132
00:10:08,075 --> 00:10:09,541
- Yeah, John.
- John.

133
00:10:09,542 --> 00:10:10,943
This is my wife, Sera.

134
00:10:10,944 --> 00:10:12,244
- Sera, lovely to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.

135
00:10:12,245 --> 00:10:13,713
- Thank you for having us.
- Oh, of course.

136
00:10:13,714 --> 00:10:15,614
Please, let's get you inside.

137
00:10:15,615 --> 00:10:16,917
[John chuckles]

138
00:10:23,791 --> 00:10:25,157
[Emmett] All right, here we are.

139
00:10:25,158 --> 00:10:27,093
You don't really see
Jacobethan Revival like this

140
00:10:27,094 --> 00:10:28,260
too much anymore.

141
00:10:28,261 --> 00:10:29,829
[Emmett] Well, well, well.

142
00:10:29,830 --> 00:10:31,463
A man who knows
his architecture.

143
00:10:31,464 --> 00:10:33,099
[John laughs] Yeah.

144
00:10:33,100 --> 00:10:35,467
So when was it built?
Early '20s?

145
00:10:35,468 --> 00:10:36,603
1918.

146
00:10:37,470 --> 00:10:41,007
When I first took possession,
she was in sore need of repair.

147
00:10:41,008 --> 00:10:43,542
I spent the better part
of a decade renovating her,

148
00:10:43,543 --> 00:10:46,947
but finally managed
to get her restored.

149
00:10:47,881 --> 00:10:50,283
Oh, it's perfect.

150
00:10:52,119 --> 00:10:54,586
- It is kind of cool, isn't it?
- [John] It is.

151
00:10:54,587 --> 00:10:56,056
[all chuckle]

152
00:10:57,224 --> 00:10:58,524
[Emmett]
So, Jeannie tells me you guys

153
00:10:58,525 --> 00:11:00,359
are looking to buy in
our neck of the woods?

154
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:03,529
Uh, yes. "Looking-looking" being
the operative word there, yes.

155
00:11:03,530 --> 00:11:06,598
We really, really love it here,
but we keep striking out.

156
00:11:06,599 --> 00:11:08,935
Doesn't surprise me.

157
00:11:08,936 --> 00:11:11,137
Real estate around here
is tricky business.

158
00:11:11,138 --> 00:11:13,572
Most of the homes, they get
passed down generationally.

159
00:11:13,573 --> 00:11:17,309
So, you're-you're what? You're
sort of an inside man? Is that it?

160
00:11:17,310 --> 00:11:18,711
- Something like that.
- Got it.

161
00:11:18,712 --> 00:11:20,279
- Okay.
- No, I have a talent

162
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:22,360
for pairing the right property
with the right people.

163
00:11:23,016 --> 00:11:27,319
And to do that, I try to get to
know the people that I'm helping.

164
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:28,487
Right.

165
00:11:28,488 --> 00:11:30,689
I just recently got my hands
on a '98 Margaux,

166
00:11:30,690 --> 00:11:32,859
and I'd hate to drink it alone.

167
00:11:32,860 --> 00:11:36,864
Okay, might I convince you
to stay for an early dinner?

168
00:11:37,530 --> 00:11:40,267
I thought maybe we could mix
a little business with pleasure.

169
00:11:42,702 --> 00:11:44,670
Yes, yes, we'd love to.

170
00:11:44,671 --> 00:11:46,906
- Terrific.
- [John] Yes.

171
00:11:46,907 --> 00:11:48,675
The dining room's right in here.

172
00:11:55,082 --> 00:11:58,785
[Emmett] <i>So, an architect
and a mathematician.</i>

173
00:11:58,786 --> 00:12:01,220
- A seeker...
- Yes.

174
00:12:01,221 --> 00:12:03,189
- And a solver.
- [Sera] Mm-hmm.

175
00:12:03,190 --> 00:12:05,258
Infinite possibilities
on one hand.

176
00:12:06,326 --> 00:12:08,227
A single solution on the other.

177
00:12:08,228 --> 00:12:10,696
What I'd like to know
is how you two work that out.

178
00:12:10,697 --> 00:12:13,766
- [all chuckle] - Well,
I'll put it to you this way.

179
00:12:13,767 --> 00:12:15,667
- [Emmett] Mm-hmm.
- I'm an impossible know-it-all...

180
00:12:15,668 --> 00:12:17,569
- [Emmett] Ah. - ...and this
one here is always right.

181
00:12:17,570 --> 00:12:19,939
- [Emmett chuckles] - You
know, between the two of us,

182
00:12:19,940 --> 00:12:22,441
- we work it out.
- Got it.

183
00:12:22,442 --> 00:12:25,678
So, why move
all the way out here?

184
00:12:26,579 --> 00:12:27,647
I mean, you're young.

185
00:12:28,515 --> 00:12:31,118
I would assume the city
would have more appeal for you.

186
00:12:33,954 --> 00:12:36,422
Well, we're leaving
one story behind.

187
00:12:36,423 --> 00:12:38,191
We're here to write a new one.

188
00:12:40,627 --> 00:12:42,228
Hmm.

189
00:12:42,229 --> 00:12:43,830
Well, then... [inhales deeply]

190
00:12:43,831 --> 00:12:46,499
Here's to this next chapter
in all our lives.

191
00:12:47,634 --> 00:12:49,936
- [John] Cheers. Cheers.
- [Sera] Cheers.

192
00:12:49,937 --> 00:12:51,171
Cheers.

193
00:12:58,411 --> 00:12:59,980
What's, uh, what's all this?

194
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:04,416
When they originally laid
the foundation for the house,

195
00:13:04,417 --> 00:13:07,854
- the workers unearthed numerous
old artifacts. - [John] Huh.

196
00:13:07,855 --> 00:13:12,658
[Emmett] Mining tools, old
military weapons, what have you.

197
00:13:12,659 --> 00:13:17,264
It was all stored in a shed
behind the house until I moved in.

198
00:13:18,065 --> 00:13:20,533
Once the renovations began,

199
00:13:21,234 --> 00:13:23,136
I myself discovered

200
00:13:23,937 --> 00:13:26,438
numerous other remnants
that had been hidden away

201
00:13:26,439 --> 00:13:28,975
in the chimneys and the walls.

202
00:13:28,976 --> 00:13:31,077
What are we talking about here?
A suitcase full of money?

203
00:13:31,078 --> 00:13:32,378
[Emmett and John chuckle]

204
00:13:32,379 --> 00:13:34,546
- Nothing that exciting.
- No? That's too bad.

205
00:13:34,547 --> 00:13:37,583
But I just couldn't bring myself
to throw any of it away,

206
00:13:37,584 --> 00:13:41,221
so I turned it
into this showcase.

207
00:13:42,655 --> 00:13:46,158
I like to think of it
as a living record of the house

208
00:13:46,159 --> 00:13:48,295
and its rich history.

209
00:13:49,129 --> 00:13:50,863
- It's really cool.
- [Emmett] Yeah.

210
00:13:50,864 --> 00:13:54,733
That's what we're looking for,
you know, a house with character.

211
00:13:54,734 --> 00:13:57,604
Ah, you know what they say
about old houses.

212
00:13:58,105 --> 00:13:59,272
They're like people.

213
00:14:00,773 --> 00:14:03,876
They all have
their deep, dark secrets.

214
00:14:03,877 --> 00:14:05,078
[Sera] Mm.

215
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:09,481
So can I please have a standing
playdate with this house?

216
00:14:09,482 --> 00:14:12,885
- [John chuckles] - Well, I don't know.
It depends. I mean...

217
00:14:12,886 --> 00:14:14,587
Well, what if the house
were yours?

218
00:14:15,722 --> 00:14:17,056
Should we make an offer?

219
00:14:17,057 --> 00:14:19,091
Yeah, I got, like,
27 bucks on me.

220
00:14:19,092 --> 00:14:20,592
Well, he'd be crazy
not to take it.

221
00:14:20,593 --> 00:14:22,561
- He would be crazy...
- I would, but let's forget

222
00:14:22,562 --> 00:14:26,498
the financial facts for a moment
and just for the sake of argument...

223
00:14:26,499 --> 00:14:28,200
[John] Yeah?

224
00:14:28,201 --> 00:14:30,203
What if this house
were yours?

225
00:14:31,571 --> 00:14:33,305
I-I'm sorry, I don't follow.

226
00:14:33,306 --> 00:14:37,009
You just said you were looking
to start a new chapter.

227
00:14:37,010 --> 00:14:38,110
- Yeah.
- Okay.

228
00:14:38,111 --> 00:14:41,314
So, my question is,

229
00:14:42,916 --> 00:14:45,284
can a house make you happy?

230
00:14:45,285 --> 00:14:49,556
Can this house make you happy?

231
00:14:54,928 --> 00:14:56,029
No, no.

232
00:14:57,230 --> 00:15:01,633
It's not the house that makes you happy.
It's the... it's the home.

233
00:15:01,634 --> 00:15:02,869
[Emmett] Hmm.

234
00:15:10,377 --> 00:15:13,680
[Sera] How about you?
Has this house made you happy?

235
00:15:15,983 --> 00:15:17,717
It did. Once.

236
00:15:18,485 --> 00:15:21,454
[John] "It did once"? What-what happened?
What changed?

237
00:15:25,158 --> 00:15:30,830
I guess my home
has become just a house.

238
00:15:33,666 --> 00:15:35,402
Just a house.

239
00:15:36,336 --> 00:15:38,505
[Emmett sighs]

240
00:15:39,672 --> 00:15:41,808
Maybe it'll be different
for the two of you.

241
00:15:46,579 --> 00:15:48,580
You've been incredibly generous,
but we should really get going.

242
00:15:48,581 --> 00:15:50,316
- Yeah.
- What kind of a host would I be

243
00:15:50,317 --> 00:15:52,884
if I didn't let my guests
stay here

244
00:15:52,885 --> 00:15:54,353
after a night of revelry, hm?

245
00:15:54,354 --> 00:15:57,223
- [chuckles] Well...
- Oh, please, listen.

246
00:15:57,224 --> 00:16:01,427
You must stay. The guest rooms are
already made upstairs. I insist.

247
00:16:01,428 --> 00:16:03,130
You really shouldn't drive.

248
00:16:03,896 --> 00:16:06,365
- I mean, I probably shouldn't drive at this point.
- No, you shouldn't.

249
00:16:06,366 --> 00:16:08,400
It's settled. [clears throat]

250
00:16:08,401 --> 00:16:10,402
[clicks tongue]
I'm gonna close up down here.

251
00:16:10,403 --> 00:16:12,504
You two have a restful sleep,

252
00:16:12,505 --> 00:16:14,940
and we continue talking
in the morning

253
00:16:14,941 --> 00:16:19,279
because I think I may know
the perfect house for you guys.

254
00:16:24,384 --> 00:16:27,319
[both laughing]

255
00:16:27,320 --> 00:16:30,090
Think about it.
Think about it, okay? Like...

256
00:16:31,058 --> 00:16:34,426
- Oh, having breakfast in the gazebo.
- Mm-hmm.

257
00:16:34,427 --> 00:16:37,796
- Throwing amazing parties.
- Mm-hmm.

258
00:16:37,797 --> 00:16:40,332
Watching our kids play
in the yard.

259
00:16:40,333 --> 00:16:43,169
- Eh, I mean, maybe a little.
- Shut up.

260
00:16:43,170 --> 00:16:46,573
[chuckles] Do you think
he can really help?

261
00:16:49,409 --> 00:16:50,810
Yeah, maybe he can.

262
00:16:53,813 --> 00:16:54,914
What?

263
00:16:56,349 --> 00:16:57,550
I missed you.

264
00:16:59,586 --> 00:17:02,755
[romantic music plays]

265
00:17:30,317 --> 00:17:32,352
[music fades]

266
00:17:34,020 --> 00:17:36,456
[car engine starts]

267
00:17:41,928 --> 00:17:42,995
[John] Do you hear that?

268
00:17:47,667 --> 00:17:49,636
Yeah, I don't know.
I think he might have left.

269
00:17:51,971 --> 00:17:53,305
[Sera sighs]

270
00:17:53,306 --> 00:17:54,806
[whispers] I don't know.

271
00:17:54,807 --> 00:17:55,808
Emmett?

272
00:18:03,216 --> 00:18:05,852
Emmett, we're taking off.
We just want to say goodbye.

273
00:18:11,324 --> 00:18:12,658
- Where is he?
- I don't know.

274
00:18:12,659 --> 00:18:15,362
[John breathes heavily]

275
00:18:16,263 --> 00:18:18,465
[John chuckles lightly]

276
00:18:20,533 --> 00:18:22,000
[John] "Dear John and Sera..."

277
00:18:22,001 --> 00:18:25,404
[Emmett] <i>It has been a pleasure
meeting you both,</i>

278
00:18:25,405 --> 00:18:27,639
<i>and in the short time
we spent together,</i>

279
00:18:27,640 --> 00:18:30,041
<i>we've shared
a meaningful connection.</i>

280
00:18:30,042 --> 00:18:34,180
<i>You're a lovely couple with
long, happy lives ahead of you.</i>

281
00:18:34,181 --> 00:18:37,749
<i>In getting to know you,
it's clear you seek change,</i>

282
00:18:37,750 --> 00:18:40,619
<i>a new beginning,
and I'd like to help.</i>

283
00:18:40,620 --> 00:18:44,190
<i>This house represents a past
I wish to leave behind.</i>

284
00:18:44,191 --> 00:18:48,561
My wife used to sit
on that patio every evening,

285
00:18:49,762 --> 00:18:51,998
just to watch the sun go down.

286
00:18:52,499 --> 00:18:54,833
<i>Since I no longer want it,</i>

287
00:18:54,834 --> 00:18:58,638
<i>I'd like to give the house
and everything in it to you.</i>

288
00:19:00,139 --> 00:19:05,377
<i>I do, however,
have one simple condition.</i>

289
00:19:05,378 --> 00:19:08,114
<i>You must never open
the cellar door.</i>

290
00:19:12,852 --> 00:19:16,556
<i>If you observe that condition,
the house is yours to keep.</i>

291
00:19:18,758 --> 00:19:20,626
<i>If you break the contract,
however,</i>

292
00:19:20,627 --> 00:19:23,463
<i>the house returns to me
immediately.</i>

293
00:19:25,398 --> 00:19:27,366
<i>I'm sure this is
but a small price to pay</i>

294
00:19:27,367 --> 00:19:29,168
<i>for the chapter
you wish to write.</i>

295
00:19:30,370 --> 00:19:32,071
<i>The decision is yours.</i>

296
00:19:33,640 --> 00:19:35,875
[John] "Sincerely, Emmett."

297
00:19:37,310 --> 00:19:39,812
[birds chirping]

298
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:49,789
- [thudding]
- [John exhales sharply]

299
00:19:50,757 --> 00:19:52,291
I just, I-I don't understand.

300
00:19:52,292 --> 00:19:55,060
What could be so unimportant,
you just would leave it behind,

301
00:19:55,061 --> 00:19:57,529
but so important,
you wouldn't want us to see it?

302
00:19:57,530 --> 00:19:58,664
Who cares?

303
00:19:58,665 --> 00:20:00,832
- Who cares?
- Yeah, who cares?

304
00:20:00,833 --> 00:20:05,404
This... Look at this place.
This-this could be ours for nothing.

305
00:20:05,405 --> 00:20:07,038
Sera, it's not nothing.

306
00:20:07,039 --> 00:20:08,740
- I don't see the big deal.
- [Jeannie] Yoo-hoo!

307
00:20:08,741 --> 00:20:10,008
Morning, neighbors.

308
00:20:10,009 --> 00:20:12,210
Oh, you guys hit the jackpot
or what?

309
00:20:12,211 --> 00:20:14,880
Jeannie, wha-wha... what is this?
Is this a joke? What's going on?

310
00:20:14,881 --> 00:20:16,648
No, no, no,
it's not a joke at all.

311
00:20:16,649 --> 00:20:19,518
I handle all of Mr. Claymore's
real estate affairs.

312
00:20:19,519 --> 00:20:21,487
I'm here
to collect the paperwork.

313
00:20:21,488 --> 00:20:23,188
- Did you sign?
- No, not yet.

314
00:20:23,189 --> 00:20:24,556
We're discussing it.

315
00:20:24,557 --> 00:20:26,859
- We're discussing this, actually.
- Oh, well...

316
00:20:28,127 --> 00:20:31,197
- We can handle that.
- Good, because that is a nonstarter.

317
00:20:31,931 --> 00:20:34,233
- Got it. Can I talk to you for a second, please?
- Yes.

318
00:20:36,703 --> 00:20:38,203
[Sera] <i>Oh, come on,
you-you were the one that said</i>

319
00:20:38,204 --> 00:20:40,506
that when the perfect thing
came along, that we would know.

320
00:20:40,507 --> 00:20:42,508
Oh, this is not
what I was talking about.

321
00:20:42,509 --> 00:20:43,942
Sera, come on.

322
00:20:43,943 --> 00:20:46,878
Aren't you just
even a little bit creeped out?

323
00:20:46,879 --> 00:20:49,448
I mean, ask yourself, why?
Why would he give it away?

324
00:20:49,449 --> 00:20:52,284
[Sera] Let's think about this
logically for a-a second, okay?

325
00:20:52,285 --> 00:20:55,020
If we buy a cheap house just so
we can get out of our apartment,

326
00:20:55,021 --> 00:20:56,655
we're gonna have to renovate
that house.

327
00:20:56,656 --> 00:20:58,424
That's gonna take, what,
like a year, 18 months?

328
00:20:58,425 --> 00:21:01,126
And then there's the-the... the
permits and the construction.

329
00:21:01,127 --> 00:21:02,629
And then where are we?

330
00:21:03,830 --> 00:21:05,465
And then there's this.

331
00:21:06,633 --> 00:21:11,037
We're just in. [chuckles] We're done.
There's-there's no stress.

332
00:21:13,272 --> 00:21:17,409
Baby, we-we sank
all of our savings into IVF.

333
00:21:17,410 --> 00:21:20,112
If we have any hope
of trying again.

334
00:21:21,848 --> 00:21:26,585
I slept for the first time
in weeks. So did you.

335
00:21:26,586 --> 00:21:28,087
Yeah, I did, but...

336
00:21:29,556 --> 00:21:32,325
it is just...
It doesn't make any sense.

337
00:21:33,292 --> 00:21:37,029
It's always gonna be you and me,
whatever we decide. Together.

338
00:21:39,732 --> 00:21:41,768
But, baby, we can heal here.

339
00:21:44,303 --> 00:21:46,739
[light piano music plays]

340
00:21:49,476 --> 00:21:51,977
- All right, thank you so much.
- You are gonna be so very happy here.

341
00:21:51,978 --> 00:21:54,245
- [Sera laughs] - You are
gonna be happy here. Yes.

342
00:21:54,246 --> 00:21:55,847
<i>And if there's anything
at all you need, call me.</i>

343
00:21:55,848 --> 00:21:57,048
[Sera] <i>Yes, I have your number.</i>

344
00:21:58,818 --> 00:22:00,185
Thank you. [light chuckle]

345
00:22:00,186 --> 00:22:02,220
- [Sera] <i>Emailed, yes.</i>
- [Jeannie] <i>All right.</i>

346
00:22:02,221 --> 00:22:04,055
- Thank you. Oh, good luck with everything.
- Oh, okay, Jeannie, bye.

347
00:22:04,056 --> 00:22:06,893
- Oh, golly. Good luck. Bye.
- Thank you. Bye.

348
00:22:08,428 --> 00:22:09,761
- This place is ours.
- [laughing]

349
00:22:09,762 --> 00:22:11,530
This is ours!

350
00:22:11,531 --> 00:22:13,164
[both laughing]

351
00:22:13,165 --> 00:22:16,001
- [John] Oh, boy...
- Oh, my God.

352
00:22:16,002 --> 00:22:17,303
[kisses]

353
00:22:21,708 --> 00:22:23,075
Oh, my God.

354
00:22:27,847 --> 00:22:31,017
[light piano music continues]

355
00:22:38,190 --> 00:22:40,259
[exhales deeply]

356
00:22:46,433 --> 00:22:49,836
[breathes deeply]

357
00:22:52,439 --> 00:22:54,205
[light chuckle]

358
00:22:54,206 --> 00:22:55,341
Hmm...

359
00:22:56,743 --> 00:22:59,746
[Sera exhales deeply] Hey.

360
00:23:00,580 --> 00:23:02,514
Oh, those don't go in here.

361
00:23:02,515 --> 00:23:03,915
- These don't go in here?
- Uh-uh.

362
00:23:03,916 --> 00:23:06,051
They go in the office.

363
00:23:06,052 --> 00:23:07,619
Oh, is that why
they say "office" on them?

364
00:23:07,620 --> 00:23:09,288
- Yeah.
- Okay.

365
00:23:11,658 --> 00:23:13,059
[John chuckles softly]

366
00:23:14,160 --> 00:23:16,996
- [music fades]
- [crickets chirping]

367
00:23:22,469 --> 00:23:25,638
[piano playing]

368
00:24:02,509 --> 00:24:05,444
[piano continues playing]

369
00:24:25,397 --> 00:24:29,201
[piano continues playing]

370
00:24:39,546 --> 00:24:41,047
[piano stops]

371
00:24:42,682 --> 00:24:45,852
[chill music plays]

372
00:24:50,089 --> 00:24:53,793
[indistinct chattering]

373
00:25:01,568 --> 00:25:04,169
[both laughing]

374
00:25:04,170 --> 00:25:06,204
- [Sera] So happy you guys are here.
- The house is great.

375
00:25:06,205 --> 00:25:07,573
- [John] Oh, thank you. Yeah.
- [Sera] Thank you.

376
00:25:07,574 --> 00:25:08,974
Well, if you need a refill,
um, booze is over there.

377
00:25:08,975 --> 00:25:10,542
- [woman] Oh, perfect. Awesome.
- Yes.

378
00:25:10,543 --> 00:25:12,077
- [woman] All right, see you in a bit.
- [John] See ya.

379
00:25:12,078 --> 00:25:13,078
- [Sera] Yeah, yeah.
- Are you kidding me?

380
00:25:13,079 --> 00:25:14,245
This place is incredible.

381
00:25:14,246 --> 00:25:15,881
Oh, thank you.
Yeah, it's pretty great.

382
00:25:15,882 --> 00:25:17,382
- Uh, you remember my wife, Sera?
- [Sera] Hi.

383
00:25:17,383 --> 00:25:19,117
- Yeah, of course.
- Hi. Good to see you.

384
00:25:19,118 --> 00:25:22,020
Hey, I'm sorry we're late. Uh...

385
00:25:22,021 --> 00:25:23,255
- [Alyssa] Hi.
- Hi.

386
00:25:24,891 --> 00:25:26,424
Hi, I'm Alyssa.
I work with John.

387
00:25:26,425 --> 00:25:30,762
Yes. Sera. Yes, I think
we met at a company party.

388
00:25:30,763 --> 00:25:32,564
I got you a housewarming gift.

389
00:25:32,565 --> 00:25:34,165
It's jasmine. It's my favorite.

390
00:25:34,166 --> 00:25:37,236
Oh, that's very sweet of you.
Thank you.

391
00:25:39,271 --> 00:25:41,439
You guys,
please go grab some drinks.

392
00:25:41,440 --> 00:25:43,409
- After you.
- Mm.

393
00:25:46,245 --> 00:25:47,446
Here, hold it.

394
00:25:49,582 --> 00:25:51,449
- Hey.
- [Alyssa] Hi.

395
00:25:51,450 --> 00:25:52,884
What are you doing here?

396
00:25:52,885 --> 00:25:54,085
Well, Zac invited me.

397
00:25:54,086 --> 00:25:56,154
What was I supposed to do,
turn him down?

398
00:25:56,155 --> 00:25:58,256
[exhales] Uh, yeah, well...

399
00:25:58,257 --> 00:26:01,027
Well, you told me
I should move on, so...

400
00:26:02,995 --> 00:26:05,864
- Besides, I wanted to see you.
- [sighs]

401
00:26:05,865 --> 00:26:08,467
I heard about what happened
and I was worried.

402
00:26:09,636 --> 00:26:11,370
I'm okay. I'm okay.

403
00:26:12,471 --> 00:26:13,471
Okay.

404
00:26:14,941 --> 00:26:16,375
Well, you know I'm here for you.

405
00:26:17,509 --> 00:26:18,778
For whatever you might need.

406
00:26:19,712 --> 00:26:23,414
You just have to understand, the
last time, it was the last time.

407
00:26:23,415 --> 00:26:26,953
And what I need is for you
to leave me alone, okay?

408
00:26:28,554 --> 00:26:30,021
[John sighs]

409
00:26:30,022 --> 00:26:31,257
[Steven] John?

410
00:26:32,091 --> 00:26:33,224
- John?
- Yeah?

411
00:26:33,225 --> 00:26:34,893
You need to come outside
right now.

412
00:26:34,894 --> 00:26:38,097
[tense music plays]

413
00:26:42,301 --> 00:26:43,269
[door closes]

414
00:26:43,270 --> 00:26:44,736
[guest 1 whispers]
What's going on?

415
00:26:44,737 --> 00:26:47,472
[indistinct whispering]

416
00:26:47,473 --> 00:26:49,175
Who is this?

417
00:26:51,678 --> 00:26:53,078
I-I think
we should call the police.

418
00:26:53,079 --> 00:26:54,513
No, no, no, I got it.

419
00:26:58,718 --> 00:26:59,952
Can I help you, man?

420
00:27:01,187 --> 00:27:03,555
Hey, buddy.
What's going on? You lost?

421
00:27:05,624 --> 00:27:07,093
What's going on?

422
00:27:09,595 --> 00:27:10,997
[soft chuckle]

423
00:27:12,899 --> 00:27:14,100
I'm sorry.

424
00:27:15,634 --> 00:27:17,336
It's okay, it's okay.

425
00:27:20,940 --> 00:27:23,542
You gotta burn it down
before it's too late.

426
00:27:24,944 --> 00:27:26,145
What?

427
00:27:27,379 --> 00:27:30,181
You gotta burn it down
before it's too late.

428
00:27:30,182 --> 00:27:32,984
It's not worth the price.

429
00:27:32,985 --> 00:27:34,620
It will destroy you.

430
00:27:38,958 --> 00:27:42,128
[tense music continues]

431
00:27:56,843 --> 00:27:58,543
[Steven]
All right, show's over, folks.

432
00:27:58,544 --> 00:28:00,947
[guest 2]
Did anyone call the police?

433
00:28:14,260 --> 00:28:15,626
Oh, hey.

434
00:28:15,627 --> 00:28:16,862
- Hi.
- Hi.

435
00:28:16,863 --> 00:28:18,629
Good morning.

436
00:28:18,630 --> 00:28:19,931
You must be the new neighbor.

437
00:28:19,932 --> 00:28:21,199
- Yes.
- Kathryn Conrad.

438
00:28:21,200 --> 00:28:22,934
- Hi, Sera Winter.
- Nice to meet you.

439
00:28:22,935 --> 00:28:24,002
We're right over there.

440
00:28:24,003 --> 00:28:25,370
- Oh, great.
- Yeah.

441
00:28:25,371 --> 00:28:27,005
I've been meaning to come by
and say hi in person,

442
00:28:27,006 --> 00:28:29,707
but my husband said I should
let you guys settle in first.

443
00:28:29,708 --> 00:28:32,343
- [both laugh] - You both
have to come over for drinks.

444
00:28:32,344 --> 00:28:34,512
- Yeah, yeah, we'd love to.
- Yeah? Okay, great.

445
00:28:34,513 --> 00:28:36,647
- I'm gonna reach out with some times.
- Okay.

446
00:28:36,648 --> 00:28:38,216
Oh, God, you seem so nice.

447
00:28:38,217 --> 00:28:39,885
I hope you guys stick around.

448
00:28:39,886 --> 00:28:41,087
Yeah.

449
00:28:43,655 --> 00:28:44,856
[John] Hey. Morning.

450
00:28:44,857 --> 00:28:48,659
[tense music plays]

451
00:28:48,660 --> 00:28:50,496
[sighs]

452
00:28:57,703 --> 00:28:59,538
[sighs]

453
00:29:02,208 --> 00:29:03,441
[Kiah] Hey, John.

454
00:29:03,442 --> 00:29:04,442
Yeah?

455
00:29:04,743 --> 00:29:07,112
HR's waiting for you
in the other conference room.

456
00:29:07,113 --> 00:29:09,048
Great, thank you.

457
00:29:09,548 --> 00:29:11,917
[John] <i>Sexual harassment?
Are you serious?</i>

458
00:29:11,918 --> 00:29:13,552
Goddamn it.

459
00:29:14,520 --> 00:29:16,387
I cannot believe
I'm gonna lose my job over this.

460
00:29:16,388 --> 00:29:19,157
Look, hey, it's just
a temporary suspension, okay?

461
00:29:19,158 --> 00:29:20,225
Just until they complete
their little investigation.

462
00:29:20,226 --> 00:29:21,326
Are you kidding me?

463
00:29:21,327 --> 00:29:22,794
We both know how this goes.

464
00:29:22,795 --> 00:29:24,863
- It's her word against mine.
- I know, I know. I...

465
00:29:24,864 --> 00:29:28,067
I mean, if I'm such a threat,
why is she coming to my house?

466
00:29:28,868 --> 00:29:30,568
- Why?
- [thuds]

467
00:29:30,569 --> 00:29:33,505
[Steven] That's all well and true,
but you got a bigger problem.

468
00:29:34,807 --> 00:29:36,308
What are you gonna tell Sera?

469
00:29:40,880 --> 00:29:44,050
[tense music continues]

470
00:30:00,066 --> 00:30:01,099
[door closes]

471
00:30:01,100 --> 00:30:04,303
[piano music plays]

472
00:30:05,671 --> 00:30:07,273
- Hey.
- Hey.

473
00:30:08,941 --> 00:30:10,575
- How you doing?
- Good.

474
00:30:10,576 --> 00:30:11,776
What's all this?

475
00:30:11,777 --> 00:30:13,444
I'm going back to work.

476
00:30:13,445 --> 00:30:15,546
You're going back to work?

477
00:30:15,547 --> 00:30:17,382
Wow, that's amazing.

478
00:30:17,383 --> 00:30:19,050
Yeah, it is,
and it's a good thing too,

479
00:30:19,051 --> 00:30:21,352
because if I had to spend another
day whiling away the hours

480
00:30:21,353 --> 00:30:23,489
in this empty house,
I might kill one of us.

481
00:30:24,090 --> 00:30:25,123
Most likely you.

482
00:30:25,124 --> 00:30:27,192
[chuckling]

483
00:30:27,193 --> 00:30:29,328
What about you?
How was your day?

484
00:30:32,564 --> 00:30:35,301
Yeah, it was-it was good.
You know, it was good.

485
00:30:37,069 --> 00:30:38,237
Good.

486
00:30:41,540 --> 00:30:44,409
- [exhales]
- [door closes]

487
00:30:44,410 --> 00:30:47,613
